llvm-3.7.1 forward port: the inliner credits the call instruction it removes

This patch CHANGES INLINING at every call site: the cost of inlining starts
30 lower (InlineConstants::InstrCost + InlineConstants::CallPenalty, 5 + 25),
"the call instruction also disappears after inlining". It is the whole code
change of upstream r286814 (6df8f27c95a5, 2016-11-14, "[InlineCost] Remove
skew when calculating call costs", first released in 4.0.0; tests left out),
the one statement in CallAnalyzer::analyzeCall at the place 4.0.0 has it
(lib/Analysis/InlineCost.cpp:1255 there), and nothing else. The upstream
date is later than the game's compiler, so the base's term is read from the
base, not from the history:

Round 275C's census of the base (c275/notes_entry.txt; d0.tsv, rel.tsv,
join_all.tsv), in 3.7.1's own cost units (-pass-remarks-analysis=inline over
the module): a deleting destructor holds its complete destructor at every
cost up to 250 (349 classes) and calls it at 265 (three: 0x794470, 0x79cbc0,
0xd88690) and from 335 up (65); a callee with the inline hint (the keyword,
or an implicitly declared destructor) is inlined at 305, 315 and 325 and
called at 375. So the base's two thresholds are in (250, 265] and
(325, 375]: the stock 225 and 325 with each cost 30 lower. 3.7.1 as
released stops at 225 and 325 ("too costly (cost=325, threshold=325)" for
Object016cbd80's destructor, which the base inlines).

The row-equivalent alternative NOT taken: `-mllvm -inline-threshold=255
-mllvm -inlinehint-threshold=355`. A developer's build carries no such
options, and upstream's own term gives the same code: the game module built
with this patch and the module built by the installed 3.7.1 with the two
options are equal function for function (round 273B: 6,645 functions, 0
differ). Where the two would differ, and no call site of the module does:
the 50% single-block bonus (and the vector bonuses) scale with the
threshold, so the options inline a single-block callee up to 382 (532 with
the hint) in 3.7.1's units where the term stops at 367 (517): a single-block
callee costing 368 to 382, or 518 to 532 hinted; and an explicit
-inline-threshold replaces the optimize-for-size and cold-callee thresholds
(Inliner::getInlineThreshold), which the term leaves in force, 30 higher.

Swept alone over the game module built by the installed 3.7.1 (round 273B,
5,496 rows, 6,656 functions): 323 functions change (156 in bytes, the rest
in relocation names; 11 more in one module only); 93 rows become equal (5,297 to
5,390: the 79 deleting destructors equal at a threshold of 250 and the 14
that needed more) and none is lost.

diff -ru a/lib/Analysis/IPA/InlineCost.cpp b/lib/Analysis/IPA/InlineCost.cpp
--- a/lib/Analysis/IPA/InlineCost.cpp
+++ b/lib/Analysis/IPA/InlineCost.cpp
@@ -1106,6 +1106,8 @@
       Cost -= InlineConstants::InstrCost;
     }
   }
+  // The call instruction also disappears after inlining.
+  Cost -= InlineConstants::InstrCost + InlineConstants::CallPenalty;
 
   // If there is only one call of the function, and it has internal linkage,
   // the cost of inlining it drops dramatically.
